# LIVELITS #
# A livelit is a live GUI widget which can be inserted into expressions. #
# It elaborates to a value of some given type. #

# Invocation: #
# To invoke a livelit, insert the name of the livelit (always prefixed with ^) then space. #
# The context inspector shows which livelits are available and their elaboration type. #

# Each livelit maintains an internal model, which is not generally something the #
# programmer should need to look at. You can see the internal model by toggling #
# the livelit projector off (bottom right corner of Hazel). #

# Built-in Livelits: #
# Currently, all livelits are built in: #

# 1. ^slider #
# Elaboration Type: Int                                                     #
# Elaboration: The current value of the slider, which is between 0 and 100. #
# Internal Model: Int                                                       #
let slider_val = ^^livelit(^slider(85)) in

# 2. ^emotion #
# Elaboration Type: String                                      #
# Elaboration: Returns a String representing an emotion,        #
#              determined as follows based on the slider value: #
#                if value < 40 then "sad"                       #
#                if value > 70 then "happy"                     #
#                otherwise "neutral"                            #
# Internal Model: Int                                           #
let current_mood = ^^livelit(^emotion(0)) in

# 3. ^fumola_thunk #
# Expansion Type: whatever the annotation says; it needs one               #
# Model: (Int, String, String) -- instance, thunk name, program            #
#                                                                          #
# Wraps its program as force(<name> := thunk { ... }). That is what makes   #
# an edit incremental: re-forcing the same name reuses the thunk's history, #
# so state written in one edit is there in the next. Write `1 := 2` in one  #
# edit, then `get(1)` in the next, and the result is 2.                     #
#                                                                          #
# The thunk name is Fumola source for a symbol, parsed by Fumola itself, so #
# `myThunk, 7 and `a(`b) all work. Two thunks in one runtime must not share #
# a name, or each overwrites the other.                                     #
#                                                                          #
# 4. ^fumola_editor #
# Expansion Type: whatever the annotation says; it needs one               #
# Model: (Int, String) -- instance, program                                #
#                                                                          #
# Evaluates at the top level, with no thunk. No reuse, but bindings outlive #
# the program, and Adapton.reset() and Adapton.peekForce work -- they       #
# cannot run inside a force. Strictly more expressive than ^fumola_thunk:   #
# you can write the force(... := thunk { ... }) yourself.                   #
#                                                                          #
# Same instance id means the same runtime, so an editor can set up state    #
# that a thunk reads.                                                       #
#                                                                          #
# 5. The peek value (not a livelit) #
# A program returning a pointer produces a value carrying the reference and #
# what it points at: 1 := 2 gives a reference reading peek(1)! holding 2.   #
# Evaluation continues through it, so a reference to an Int is usable as an #
# Int, while the reference stays visible.                                   #
#                                                                          #
# Always in scope: pointer(s), get(s), peek(s), since Hazel strings cannot  #
# contain the quotes the adapton primitives need. `:=` turns its left side  #
# into a pointer but `@` does not, so read a cell back with get(s).         #
#                                                                          #
# A symbol arrives in Hazel as its text: `x becomes "x".                    #
#                                                                          #
# The Fumola module library is bound at the top level, so Gcd.gcd(12, 18)   #
# works with no import:                                                     #
#   Adapton  List  LazyList  PureList  LevelTree  HashMap                   #
#   Counters  RandomInput  MergeSort  Gcd  DelayedPut                       #
# Requires the Fumola wasm runtime; see scripts/build-fumola-wasm.sh.        #
# An example of the three together. All three name instance 0, so they      #
# share one Fumola runtime.                                                 #
#                                                                          #
# The thunk writes a cell and returns 137. The first editor asks for a      #
# pointer to that cell, which comes back as a peek value showing both the   #
# reference and what it holds -- and typing as Int, so the annotation is    #
# satisfied and evaluation continues through it. The second editor forces   #
# the thunk the first livelit made, by its name `t, which is why naming a   #
# thunk explicitly matters: you can refer to it from somewhere else.        #
#                                                                          #
# The result is (137, peek(`x)! = 274, 137).                                #
let x : Int = ^fumola_thunk(0, "`t", "`x := 137 + 137; 137") in
let x_ : Int = ^fumola_editor(0, "pointer(`x)") in
let y : Int = ^fumola_editor(0, "force(pointer(`t))") in
(x, x_, y)
